-- title:
--   The radial cutoff is bounded above by one
-- statement:
--   Work in Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$. For any centre $x_0 \in \mathbb{R}^d$, any radii $R, R' \in \mathbb{R}$ and any point $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$, the standard radial cutoff satisfies
--   $$\chi_{x_0, R, R'}(x) \le 1.$$
--
--   Together with non-negativity this confines the cutoff to the unit interval, which is what makes multiplication by $\chi$ a contraction in every pointwise sense: $|\chi f| \le |f|$, so no estimate is inflated when a function is localized. It is the routine bound invoked whenever a cutoff is inserted into an integral or a quadratic form.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Unlike the two lemmas identifying the cutoff on the inner and outer balls, this bound carries no hypothesis relating $R$ and $R'$: it holds for arbitrary real $R, R'$, including the degenerate cases $R' \le R$ where the transition region collapses or is inverted. The cutoff is thus bounded by $1$ purely by construction.
